### What is this question really testing?

Pragmatic engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to see that you make deliberate, risk-based trade-offs instead of treating quality and speed as all-or-nothing.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class QualitySpeedTest {
    enum Signal { PRAGMATIC, RECKLESS, PERFECTIONIST }
    public static Signal evaluate(boolean protectedMoneyPath, boolean shippedOnTime) {
        if (!protectedMoneyPath) return Signal.RECKLESS;
        if (!shippedOnTime) return Signal.PERFECTIONIST;
        return Signal.PRAGMATIC;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(evaluate(true, true)); // PRAGMATIC
    }
}
```

### How do you classify components by risk?

Money-handling and data-integrity components are high-risk. Cosmetic features are low-risk. Apply different quality bars accordingly.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.util.List;
public class RiskClassification {
    record Component(String name, String risk, String qualityBar) {}
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Component> components = List.of(
            new Component("Balance calculation",  "HIGH",  "Full tests + review, no exceptions"),
            new Component("Transfer list",        "HIGH",  "Full tests + review, no exceptions"),
            new Component("Spending chart",       "LOW",   "Ship fast, polish in next sprint"),
            new Component("Dashboard styling",    "LOW",   "Ship fast, polish in next sprint")
        );
        components.forEach(c -> System.out.println(c.name() + " [" + c.risk() + "] -> " + c.qualityBar()));
    }
}
```

### How do you protect money paths under deadline pressure?

Complete tests and a proper review before launch, no exceptions. Showing a customer a wrong balance is unacceptable.

```java
package com.bank.service;
public class MoneyPathProtection {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Rule: any code touching money gets full test coverage + review");
        System.out.println("Even under deadline: this is non-negotiable");
        System.out.println("A wrong balance destroys trust; a late chart does not");
    }
}
```

### How do you track deferred quality?

Record the deferred polish as explicit technical debt with an owner and a planned date. It is a deliberate trade-off, not a silent corner cut.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.util.List;
public class TechnicalDebtTracker {
    record Debt(String item, String owner, String planned) {}
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Debt> debts = List.of(
            new Debt("Spending chart responsive design", "Amit", "Sprint 12"),
            new Debt("Dashboard accessibility polish",  "Neha", "Sprint 12")
        );
        System.out.println("Tracked technical debt:");
        debts.forEach(d -> System.out.println("  " + d.item() + " [" + d.owner() + "] planned: " + d.planned()));
    }
}
```

### What was the outcome?

Launched on time at National Bank of India. Every money-related path was fully correct and tested. Cosmetic polish followed in the next iteration exactly as planned.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class QualitySpeedOutcome {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Launched: on time for marketing deadline");
        System.out.println("Money paths: fully tested, zero defects");
        System.out.println("Cosmetic polish: completed in sprint 12 as planned");
        System.out.println("Technical debt: all items resolved, none forgotten");
    }
}
```

### What should you never compromise?

Data correctness, security, and financial calculations. These are irreversible once trust is lost.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class NeverCompromise {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Never compromise: balance calculations, transfer correctness, security");
        System.out.println("OK to defer: styling, animation, nice-to-have reports");
        System.out.println("Principle: trust is irreversible; polish is not");
    }
}
```

### How is this different from cutting corners?

Cutting corners is untracked and accidental. Deliberate trade-offs are tracked, owned, and planned for resolution.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class TradeOffVsCornerCut {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Corner cut: 'We will fix it later' (no plan, no owner)");
        System.out.println("Trade-off: 'Spending chart ships rough, Amit owns polish in sprint 12'");
        System.out.println("The difference is visibility, ownership, and a plan.");
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class QualitySpeedFollowUps {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Did the debt get resolved? -> Yes, all items completed in sprint 12");
        System.out.println("What if the deadline was tighter? -> Negotiate scope, never quality on money");
        System.out.println("How do you decide the line? -> Risk-based: money = full, cosmetic = fast");
    }
}
```
